# {{ project_name }} agent workflow

## Responsibility map

- GitHub Issues and pull requests define work, progress, and evidence. Approved specs and ADRs preserve durable context; add a plan only for cross-session, high-risk, or hard-to-recover work, and never store raw chat transcripts. Follow the [`.csarc/docs/csarc.md` workflow](.csarc/docs/csarc.md#工作流程) for work-item relationships and fields.
- Root `AGENTS.md` is the discovery entry point, this file contains its single detailed CSARC workflow, and `.claude/CLAUDE.md` only imports the root entry point.
- One writable task uses one Git branch and one worktree so concurrent changes stay isolated.
{% if verification_mode == 'local' %}- Reusable validation lives in `.csarc/scripts/` and tests. Run focused checks while iterating, then run `./.csarc/scripts/verify-fast` once on the committed final candidate; it automatically promotes itself to full when path risk requires it. Successful verification records self-attested evidence bound to the clean exact head/tree, base, tier, scopes, result, and freshness. The merge lifecycle revalidates that evidence, live review and the remote lease before its audited admin bypass. This is not trusted hosted or release provenance. See working loop step 12 and [`.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md`](.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md#本機與-hosted-驗證模式908).
{% else %}- Reusable validation lives in `.csarc/scripts/` and tests. During implementation and before an ordinary push, run focused checks for the owners touched by the change; `./.csarc/scripts/verify-fast` remains an optional broad local diagnostic. Reserve `./.csarc/scripts/verify` for a true full delivery boundary. The required hosted `verify` check independently selects the tier from trusted base code and executes the risk-owned entry point once on the exact candidate; `.csarc/scripts/check-trusted-verification` binds that GitHub-hosted result to the repository, commit/tree, tier, scopes, command, toolchain, runner, result, and freshness. Local success is useful contributor feedback but never substitutes for the hosted check. Dependabot follows this same path with no bot-specific bypass. See working loop step 12 for the full-tier once-per-PR rule. Checked-in files under `.github/workflows/` are the only active GitHub Actions. See [`.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md`](.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md#驗證分級與實測成本) for the tier boundary and stage ownership.
{% endif %}
- [`.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md`](.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md#審查與合併資格) owns review requirements, merge eligibility, configured admin bypass, and quota fallback. Do not restate or invent exceptions here.
- Copier owns template updates. Follow the [`.csarc/docs/csarc.md` update contract](.csarc/docs/csarc.md#公版更新).

## Scope and sources of truth

- This file applies repository-wide. A nearer `AGENTS.md` may add subtree-specific guidance.
- `.csarc/config.yml` is the template-managed source for enabled language and repository modules.
- Use the linked Issue or `docs/specs/` SDD contract for scope; `docs/README.md` maps durable project memory.
- Root `SECURITY.md` is repository-wide scanner policy; `.github/SECURITY.md` is the human vulnerability-disclosure policy. See [`.csarc/docs/security-scanner-readiness.md`](.csarc/docs/security-scanner-readiness.md) for provider capabilities, exact validation commands, scope, cost, and offline boundaries.
- Use `docs/adr/` for decisions. On updated projects, also search project-owned `docs/decisions/`; never move or overwrite it automatically.
- Edit project site content in `docs/site/content/_index.zh-tw.md` / `_index.en.md` and theme overrides in `docs/site/theme.css`, then rebuild with `./.csarc/scripts/build-repo-site`; do not hand-edit the generated `docs/index.html` / `docs/index.en.html`.

## Working loop

1. At task start, run only `git worktree list --porcelain`; reserve unscoped cleanup for explicit maintenance. Creator cleanup after merge remains required. Before planning or opening an Issue or Milestone, list open Milestones and open Issues, then search open and closed Issues with two to four concrete problem or technology terms. Inspect the body, comments, and linked pull requests of credible matches; titles and labels alone are not decisions. Keep each query bounded to 20 candidates.
2. Use the [work-item model](.csarc/docs/csarc.md#工作流程). A Milestone is an optional dated delivery or release bucket; follow `.csarc/docs/milestone-description.md`. Projects stay disabled.
3. Before creating the work item, present a short prior-decision summary when the user can respond. If creation is already authorized, record each relevant `#N` as preserved, superseded, or rejected with a reason in the Issue supplement or Milestone `References`; when none are found, record the bounded queries used. When a user confirms a durable architecture, tooling, security, compatibility, or platform constraint, summarize it in the work item and update the matching `docs/adr/` record through the scoped pull request. Never store a raw conversation transcript or promote an unconfirmed inference to a decision. Never silently reverse an earlier decision.
4. Use the checked-in Issue form and pull request template whether creating through the UI, CLI, or API; do not create a second body format.
5. Open or select one GitHub Issue before editing and use its acceptance criteria as scope. Record `beta` for Milestone work and `stable` for a Milestone tracker, standalone change, or hotfix; reject a conflicting declaration. State the required suite (channel floor plus path risk) and whether its evidence is local or hosted. Titles use 12-80 ASCII characters, at least three words, no type prefix, and no trailing period. Set one native Type, matching label, assignee, and applicable parent and Milestone. Use `.csarc/scripts/gh-issue-edit` instead of raw `gh issue edit` for body changes so an existing approval cannot be invalidated without a local warning; the wrapper is advisory and cannot intercept GitHub UI or direct API edits. The creator is the default owner: UI creators assign themselves, while CLI and agents pass `--assignee @me`; change the assignee only for an explicit handoff.
6. If new work exceeds that Issue, stop and open a separate Issue; do not silently widen the current branch or pull request. When that new Issue surfaces from a problem discovered while working a Milestone Issue, default to keeping it inside that same Milestone (Milestone-tagged, based on its `dev/m<N>` delivery branch) rather than opening it standalone. Open it standalone only when it clearly meets one of: it spans multiple Milestones or is a governance/tooling mechanism affecting all future Milestones, not this Milestone's own feature scope; it is an urgent production fix that cannot wait for the Milestone to close; or it originates from an external report (another collaborator or peer session) with no direct causal link to this Milestone's own work. When unsure which applies, default to keeping it in the Milestone — the exceptions are narrow, not a default escape hatch.
7. Check every acceptance-criteria box in the Issue, and every pull request checklist box, only once it carries real evidence, and finish all of them before opening a pull request or pushing a commit meant to satisfy the closing keyword — not incrementally, box by box, across separate pushes. Each push re-triggers the same PR policy gate; pushing a commit you already know is incomplete guarantees that gate fails. Keep the pull request in draft while work is still incomplete: the acceptance-checklist gate does not enforce completeness on a draft, and revalidates in full the moment it is marked ready for review — see `.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md`'s "Acceptance-checklist 驗證時機（#573）" section.
8. For a Milestone Issue, start from its one short-lived `dev/m<milestone>-short-slug` delivery branch. For an Issue without a Milestone, start from current `main`; if independent changes must soak or ship together, create a real Milestone instead of a catch-all branch. Use `gh issue develop <issue> --base <base> --name type/<issue-number>-short-slug` for the native Development link, and follow `.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md` for explicitly authorized canary, synchronization, promotion, hotfix, and recovery routes. When a pull request is stacked on another work branch, retarget it to the shared integration branch as soon as the base pull request is ready to merge, before that branch is squash-merged and deleted. GitHub auto-closes (does not auto-retarget) a pull request whose base branch no longer exists; recovering it means opening a new pull request from the same head branch, which loses the original PR's number, reviews, and comment history.
9. Inspect nearby code, tests, and configuration before choosing an implementation.
10. Make the smallest coherent change; preserve unrelated work and project-owned source files.
11. Add or update the narrowest regression test that proves non-trivial behavior.
12. Delegates run scoped or focused checks while iterating. The same router selects fast or full by changed paths and delivery stage (`.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md`); docs-only work is a fast scope optimization, not another tier. {% if verification_mode == 'local' %}After the candidate is committed and clean, run `./.csarc/scripts/verify-fast` once; it records the exact local evidence consumed by the lifecycle, and a later tree or base change requires one new run. Do not run the suite a second time merely to create evidence.{% else %}The hosted `verify` check executes that risk-owned suite once on the exact candidate and provides the merge evidence. Do not duplicate that routine suite locally by default; only when the PR itself is a full-tier delivery boundary does the owner or integrator run `./.csarc/scripts/verify` once locally on the final candidate tree.{% endif %}
13. Before any automated PR Ready/Draft, authorization, metadata, or merge write, acquire the remote lease and use `.csarc/scripts/pr_lifecycle.py`; otherwise remain read-only. Follow Journey 08 rather than duplicating merge rules here.
14. After merge, leave the task worktree and run `./.csarc/scripts/cleanup-worktrees --apply --worktree <path> origin/<pull-request-base>` from another checkout. Never remove another task's worktree or any dirty, locked, detached, unmerged, or unverifiable worktree.
15. Report the changed behavior, verification evidence, and any remaining limitation.

Writable agents use one branch and worktree per independent task. Reuse a host-managed worktree; never force a branch into multiple worktrees or remove one owned by another task or with uncommitted changes. Use local storage for active checkouts, worktrees, environments, and caches. If assigned a cloud-synced File Provider path, switch to a local checkout without routine user confirmation; preserve existing changes. Worktrees isolate files; integration still uses PR, CI, review, and final verification.

Never push directly to an integration branch, invent successful evidence, create or dispatch release tags manually, or bypass the linked governance policy.

Duplicate triage may close an Issue without code changes when it links the canonical Issue and uses GitHub's native duplicate reason. If a change or `copier update` modifies an Issue or pull request body shape, migrate existing bodies before closing it; otherwise create and link a follow-up Issue first.

Existing-repository history changes start read-only: propose semantic story groups and exclusions, wait for explicit confirmation, then update Milestones and associations idempotently. Never infer groups from titles or labels alone, require every historical Issue to have a Milestone, or reopen completed Issues.

## Commands

{% if "python" in languages %}- Python setup: `uv sync --locked`.
{% endif %}{% if "typescript" in languages %}- TypeScript setup: `corepack enable && pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
{% endif %}{% if "rust" in languages %}- Rust setup: `rustup show && cargo fetch --locked`. On Linux/WSL2, compiling this project also needs a system C linker -- install `build-essential` (or the equivalent toolchain) first; a machine with only `rustup` fails with `error: linker 'cc' not found`.
{% endif %}{% if not languages %}- Environment setup: no language toolchain install is required.
{% endif %}- Site source check: `./.csarc/scripts/build-repo-site --check`.
- Security smoke: `./.csarc/scripts/security-smoke` selects four existing offline owner self-tests; it is not an AI security scan and is not part of every routine gate. See `.csarc/docs/security-scanner-readiness.md` for the exact command inventory and limits.
{% if "python" in languages %}- Python iteration: `uv run ruff check <paths>`, `uv run ty check`, and `uv run pytest <test-path>`.
{% endif %}{% if "typescript" in languages %}- TypeScript iteration: `pnpm exec biome check <paths>`, `pnpm exec tsc --noEmit`, and `pnpm exec vitest run <test-path>`.
{% endif %}{% if "rust" in languages %}- Rust iteration: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo clippy --locked --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`, and `cargo test --locked`.
{% endif %}- Focused local checks: run the narrow owner checks for touched paths before an ordinary push.
- Final verification: {% if verification_mode == 'local' %}run `./.csarc/scripts/verify-fast` once after committing; its existing router promotes full-risk work to `./.csarc/scripts/verify` and records the local evidence without another suite invocation.{% else %}hosted CI runs the selected risk-owned tier once on the exact candidate; use `./.csarc/scripts/verify-fast` locally only as an optional broad diagnostic, and run `./.csarc/scripts/verify` locally only for a full-tier delivery boundary.{% endif %}
{% if verification_mode == 'hosted' %}- Inspect trusted hosted evidence after a run: `./.csarc/scripts/check-trusted-verification <head-sha> --github-repo <owner/repo>`.
{% endif %}
{% if copilot_review == 'allowed' %}- Copilot review loop (`copilot_review: allowed` in `.csarc/config.yml`): after each push, `python3 .csarc/scripts/review_gate.py status --repo <owner/repo> --pr <N>` lists Copilot's findings on the current head and the unresolved threads; fix them locally, push, answer and resolve the threads, and repeat until Copilot's review of the exact head is clean and the `review` check passes, then merge through `.csarc/scripts/pr_lifecycle.py`. A maintainer approval of the exact head remains a valid alternative. See `.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md`'s "Copilot 審核模式（#752）" section.
{% endif %}- Release execution (if this repository owns `release.yml`): hosted Automatic/Guided publish is a known permanent limitation, not a bug to chase — `GITHUB_TOKEN` cannot prove the Immutable Releases setting and this fails closed by design. Run `.csarc/scripts/publish-release` locally under the maintainer's own admin identity instead; see `.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md`'s hosted-release-limitation section.
- Milestone preflight: `python3 .csarc/scripts/sync_milestone_state.py preflight --repo <repo> --milestone <N>` validates a Milestone's due date, tracker title, and `Lifecycle Issue: #N` link before dispatching work under it, instead of waiting for the first PR to fail `Validate Milestone approval` — see `.csarc/docs/milestone-description.md`.

## Editing boundaries

{% if not languages %}- This profile does not create a product language module. New product code requires an intentional profile change before language-specific CI is claimed.
{% else %}- Product code under {% if "python" in languages %}`src/{{ package_name }}/` and `tests/`{% endif %}{% if "typescript" in languages %}{% if "python" in languages %}, plus {% endif %}`typescript/src/` and `typescript/tests/`{% endif %}{% if "rust" in languages %}{% if "python" in languages or "typescript" in languages %}, plus {% endif %}`src/lib.rs`{% endif %} belongs to this project. Copier updates must not overwrite it.
- Do not hand-edit generated lockfiles. Use the selected language package manager so integrity metadata stays valid.
{% endif %}
- Keep third-party GitHub Actions pinned to full commit SHAs.
- Do not create a separate GitHub repository for testing or validation. Use local temporary projects or this repository's Issues, branches, pull requests, and Actions.
- Comments in source code must be written in English. Leave style details in the selected language's executable configuration instead of copying them here.

## Platform capability and automation

- Keep desired automation declarative and testable; do not assume organization or enterprise administrator access.
- Never extract or persist `gh auth token` as project configuration, require a personal token for the portable baseline, or treat a permission error as proof that a capability is available.
- Report blocked or unavailable capability explicitly. Never claim a release, deployment, review gate, or other control succeeded when it was skipped or unsupported.

## Safety

- Never commit secrets, tokens, private keys, or populated `.env` files. Use GitHub Secrets or approved secret management for automation.
- Review `.csarc/scripts/apply-repository-settings.sh plan` before `apply`; do not operate production or external infrastructure without explicit authorization.
- Do not bypass failed verification, review, CODEOWNER approval, or supply-chain controls outside the configured lifecycle. `.csarc/docs/ci-policy.md` is the only source for local self-attestation, hosted checks, and configured admin-bypass authorization.

## Code Review Rules

- Verify the change satisfies the Issue acceptance criteria and has relevant tests.
- Flag unintended public API or release behavior changes; require an explicit compatibility and SemVer decision.
- Review dependency, workflow-permission, secret-handling, and artifact-verification changes as security-sensitive.
- Prefer existing project patterns and focused changes over new abstractions or broad rewrites.

## Nested instructions

- Add a nested `AGENTS.md` only when a subtree has genuinely different commands, ownership, or safety boundaries.
- Use `AGENTS.override.md` only when replacing same-directory guidance is intentional; do not pre-create empty instruction files.
